CKF Hard Mode @VERSION@
for Cyber Knights: Flashpoint


WHAT IT IS

A difficulty overhaul. It retunes the enemy, weapon, armour, implant and
consumable tables, changes how mission rewards and mission power level are
calculated, and adds a merc fatigue system and a penalty for letting a
mission's clock run down. Every number it uses is in a config file you can
edit, and the editor in this zip is how you edit them.

It is built in slices. Each part of the mod is one file and one on/off switch,
so you can keep the parts you want and turn off the parts you don't.


INSTALL

1. Find the game folder. It is the one holding CyberKnights.exe. On Steam:
   right-click the game in your library, Manage, Browse local files.

2. Extract everything in this zip into that folder. Not into
   CyberKnights_Data, and not into a subfolder. When you are done, winhttp.dll
   and CKF-Config-Editor.exe sit next to CyberKnights.exe, with BepInEx and
   dotnet folders beside them.

That is the whole install. The config files come out of the zip with everything
else, so there is nothing to run first. Nothing is installed into Windows,
nothing runs in the background, and nothing has to be run as administrator.


UPGRADING FROM AN EARLIER VERSION -- READ THIS FIRST

Extract this zip over your game folder and let it overwrite everything. You do
not convert anything, you do not migrate anything, and you do not keep your old
settings files. You get this version's tuning.

Then do one thing by hand:

    DELETE  BepInEx\config\ckf.hardmode.json

Before or after extracting; either is fine. Delete it, don't rename it and
don't leave it in that folder under another name ending in .json.

WHY. Up to version 3, every setting lived in that one file, ckf.hardmode.json.
From version 4 each part of the mod has its own file in BepInEx\config\
ckf.hardmode.d\ instead. Extracting this zip puts the new files there -- but
extracting never DELETES anything, so your old ckf.hardmode.json is still
sitting beside them.

If both are there, the mod refuses to run. It does not pick one. Two files
holding the same settings means one of them is ignored, and quietly choosing
which of your edits count is the one thing it will not do. So it stops, writes
this into BepInEx\LogOutput.log, and the game runs completely unmodified:

    ConfigDoc: BOTH CONFIG LAYOUTS ARE ON DISK.
    Plugin: stopping here. The game runs UNMODIFIED this launch.

That is safe -- nothing is damaged and nothing is lost. But the mod does nothing
at all until you delete the old file. Delete it and relaunch.

If you were on version 4 already, there is nothing to delete. Extract over the
top and you are done.

If you had retuned settings you want to keep, copy the whole BepInEx\config
folder somewhere else BEFORE you extract. The zip's copy overwrites yours.


THE FIRST LAUNCH TAKES A WHILE

The first time you start the game with this installed, BepInEx downloads a set
of Unity library files from unity.bepinex.dev and generates the interop
assemblies it needs for your version of Unity. It takes noticeably longer than
a normal launch and the window can sit there with nothing visible happening.
Let it finish. Later launches are normal, and neither the download nor the
generation happens again unless the game updates.

That download is the only time anything here touches the network. If you are
offline or behind a strict firewall, it is the thing to allow.

If any config file did not make it out of the zip, the mod says so at the top
of the log: look in BepInEx\LogOutput.log for lines starting with "Defaults:".
It names every file it could not find. Extracting the zip's BepInEx\config
folder over the game folder again puts them back.


TURNING PARTS OF THE MOD ON AND OFF

Open BepInEx\config\ckf.hardmode.cfg in Notepad, or use the editor.

The first key, under [General], is the master switch:

    Enabled = true

Set it to false and the mod does nothing at all, without uninstalling it.

Under it, [Slices] has one switch per part of the mod -- 42 of them. Set one to
false and that part alone stops applying; everything else still runs. They are,
in the order they appear in the file:

    ConsumablesChems            chem consumables
    ConsumablesDevices          device consumables
    ConsumablesGrenades         grenade consumables
    ConsumablesMatrix           matrix consumables
    ConsumablesMedical          medical consumables
    ConsumablesSploitkits       sploitkit consumables

    CyberweaponsClaws           claw cyberweapons
    CyberweaponsLasers          eye laser cyberweapons

    Difficulty                  widens the game's own custom-difficulty
                                sliders past their stock bounds
    Elapse                      the mission elapse penalty -- what it costs to
                                let a mission's clock run down
    Fatigue                     mission fatigue: Running Empty and Off-Duty
    GearClasses                 player weapon classes -- recoil and the rest

    ImplantsGlobal              implant multipliers that apply across the board
    ImplantsSlot01 .. Slot11    implant tuning, one switch per implant slot

    MissionRewards              per-mission-type reward overrides
    ModelRules                  the declarative row-by-row game table edits
    PowerLevel                  the mission power level ceiling -- lets missions
                                generate above PL 10
    Progression                 the Team Power Level award, and the victory
                                screen labels that mirror it
    RewardCurve                 the baseline reward curve
    RuleModel                   the game's own rule constants
    SelfCheck                   OFF by default. A diagnostic that writes a
                                report and changes nothing in the game

The eleven talent switches are one per class. FIVE OF THEM ARE NAMED AFTER A
CLASS THE GAME DOES NOT HAVE. The key names are older than the class names and
they are kept exactly as they are, because renaming one would throw away the
setting you had saved under it. Read this column, not the key:

    TalentsAEX                  Agent EX
    TalentsCS                   Cybersword
    TalentsCyberKnight          Cyber Knight
    TalentsGunslinger           Gunslinger
    TalentsHacker               Hacker
    TalentsSawbones             Scourge
    TalentsSniper               Sniper
    TalentsSoldier              Soldier
    TalentsVanguard             Vanguard
    TalentsWarMachine           Warmachine
    TalentsWraith               Wireghost

Relaunch the game to apply a change. Turning a slice off leaves its file alone
-- turn it back on and your numbers are still there.


CHANGING SETTINGS

Double-click CKF-Config-Editor.exe.

It opens a page in your browser and serves it from your own machine on
127.0.0.1. Nothing is uploaded and nothing is downloaded. A console window
stays open while it runs; closing that window stops the editor. Every setting
has its documentation beside it.

Saving takes a moment. Each save re-validates the whole config in a separate
process and is refused outright if that check cannot run, so a save that
succeeds is a config the game will load.

On its first run the editor writes CKF-Config-Editor.settings.json next to
itself. It holds the path to your game folder and nothing else. Deleting it is
safe; the editor writes it again.


THE CONFIG FILES

They come out of this zip into BepInEx\config:

    ckf.hardmode.cfg           the switches: [General] Enabled, plus one
                               [Slices] key per part of the mod
    ckf.hardmode.selfcheck.csv the values the mod checks itself against
    ckf.hardmode.d\            one file per part of the mod, plus the table
                               overlays and one generated file

Everything the editor edits is in ckf.hardmode.d\. A .json there holds one
part's settings; a .csv there is a table overlay, one line per game row
changed. The editor edits both.

The mod does not write these. Delete one and it is gone: the mod reports it
missing in the log and that part of the mod has nothing to work from. The
exception is ckf.hardmode.cfg, which BepInEx owns and rewrites itself on every
launch. Extracting BepInEx\config from this zip over the game folder is the
reset button, and it puts the shipped values back over your own.

There is NO ckf.hardmode.json and NO ckf.hardmode.rules.json in this version.
If either is in BepInEx\config, it is left over from an older install -- see
UPGRADING above.


BACK UP YOUR SAVES

The fatigue and elapsed-time systems write to your save file. Before you play
with this installed, copy your saves somewhere else. They are in:

    %USERPROFILE%\AppData\LocalLow\TreseBrothersGames\CyberKnights\

Paste that into the address bar of a File Explorer window to get there.


UNINSTALL

Delete these from the game folder:

    winhttp.dll
    doorstop_config.ini
    .doorstop_version
    changelog.txt
    CKF-Config-Editor.exe
    CKF-Config-Editor.settings.json
    the whole BepInEx folder
    the whole dotnet folder

The dotnet folder comes from this zip; the game does not ship one and does not
use it.

Verifying the game files through Steam will not remove them, because none of
them are Steam's.


WHAT IS IN THIS ZIP

    BepInEx\, dotnet\, winhttp.dll, doorstop_config.ini, .doorstop_version
        BepInEx @BE_BUILD@, unmodified. It is the loader; it is not part of
        this mod. dotnet\ is the .NET runtime BepInEx runs on and is most of
        the size of this download. changelog.txt in the game folder is
        BepInEx's, not this mod's. See LICENSE-BepInEx.txt.

    BepInEx\plugins\CKFHardMode.dll
        the mod.

    BepInEx\config\
        the settings files listed above. They are loose files, not built into
        the mod, and this zip is the only place they come from.

    CKF-Config-Editor.exe
        the settings editor.

This BepInEx build is a bleeding-edge one, pinned by build number and commit,
because BepInEx 6 for IL2CPP has no stable release. Installing a different one
over the top of it is not supported.


